  THE LEGACY (ASTRA) BRANCH HANDED EVERY WORKER THE LAUNCHER'S WHOLE ENVIRONMENT. route_flags'
  legacy branch returned `dict(env or os.environ)` while the Muse branch four lines below it was
  carefully scrubbed — a guard that existed, was proved, and did not cover the path that was
  leaking. SSH_AUTH_SOCK, all six provider keys and this session's CLAUDE_CODE_MESSAGING_* reached
  every Astra worker. One strip now serves both routes: scrub_env(base, keep=None).
  SCOPE, AS THE OWNER FENCED IT: the environment only. codex_model, codex_effort and codex_slots are
  untouched, and roster.json was not opened.
  ACCEPTANCE — THE INSTALLED THING, NOT THE DIFF. A child spawned with the computed environment
  dumped its own names: 33 names, ZERO matching credentials or handles. CONTROL: the same regex
  against this launcher's environment matches 30 names including SSH_AUTH_SOCK,
  CLAUDE_CODE_MESSAGING_SOCKET/TOKEN and all six OPENCODE keys — so the zero is a measurement and
  not a broken probe.
  AND THE OTHER DIRECTION, which is the dangerous one: a REAL Astra worker spawned with the stripped
  environment ran to completion. rc=0, 13s, model gpt-6-astra, effort medium, session
  01a07fca-e423-78b3-b0ab-eb9b03e5dcda, replied OK. Astra authenticates from ~/.codex/auth.json — a
  FILE, verified present first — so it needs no credential in the environment at all. A strip that
  stops the worker starting would be worse than the leak.
  PROOF: tests/test_legacyenv.py (18 checks, new, wired). EVERY check drives route_flags(M.LEGACY) —
  the branch that was leaking; the Muse branch appears only as a control. Guard list taken FIRST and
  it was EMPTY: no existing test asserted anything about the legacy branch's environment, so the
  must-bite mutation is correctly aimed. Whole suite ALL GREEN.
  Six mutations, 6/6 CAUGHT, all restored to pre-image md5: revert :404 to dict(env or os.environ)
  (the must-bite) · stop stripping HANDLES · stop stripping CLAUDE*/SENTRY/GH_ · strip so
  aggressively PATH and HOME go too · silently change the legacy DEFAULT effort · silently change
  the legacy DEFAULT model.
  TWO FOUND WHILE SCORING, both real:
    * the scope fence was unguarded where it is weakest. Every check passed an explicit model and
      effort, so the `or "gpt-6-astra"` / `or "medium"` fallbacks never ran and a default change
      scored MISSED. A caller that passes nothing gets whatever the fallback says; now asserted.
    * `keep` did not keep. The provider-key loop ran before the keep check, so
      scrub_env(base, keep="OPENCODE_GO_KEY_2") removed the name it was told to keep. child_env
      never noticed because it re-adds the key immediately after. A parameter named `keep` that does
      not keep is a trap for the next reader. Fixed.
  ONE MUTATION WAS INVALID AND IS RECORDED AS SUCH: dropping the OPENCODE prefix from the first loop
  is byte-identical in behaviour, because the second loop's "KEY" substring catches every provider
  key anyway. Measured both ways rather than inferred. The strip is defended in depth.
  RESIDUAL, NOT IN SCOPE AND NOT CHANGED: the spawned child still carries
  CODEX_COMPANION_SESSION_ID and CODEX_COMPANION_TRANSCRIPT_PATH. Neither is a credential and
  neither is in HANDLES, but both are this session's handles by any ordinary reading. Named here so
  it is a decision somebody makes rather than something nobody noticed.
  dispatcher/ has no CI. This local green is the only evidence that will ever exist.

